# Which mill reads which file in @term/site, and — for `hook` — what a file's
# statements MEAN.
#
# `hook` spells two different things: a CLI command and a URL route. Telling
# them apart from CONTENT is a guess, and the guess was wrong: the rule looked
# for a `view` child, so an API route (`hook /users` with `task get` and
# `task post`, no view) was built as a CLI command and its methods were
# collected into the wrong shape. A file that DECLARES which it holds cannot be
# guessed wrong. (An API route is a `route` record now, not a `hook`: a page route's `task get` built nothing and is
# refused, so code/test/site/server.tree is ordinary code.)
#
#   role site    every `hook` here is a URL route
#   role call    every `hook` here is a CLI command
#
# `take` names a glob, `miss` excludes one, and `@` is the package root. This
# file is at the default location (`base/role.tree`), so @term/site's manifest
# says nothing about roles.

load @term/code
  find code

role site
  take @/code/test/site/route.tree

role call
  take @/code/test/site/console.tree

role code
  take @/code/**/*.tree
    miss @/code/test/site/route.tree
    miss @/code/test/site/console.tree
